{"work_id":"raja-rani","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source_scene_number":null,"scene_status":"verified","unit_count":27,"units":[{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u001","kind":"stage-direction","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":null,"source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":{"kind":"source-heading","ordinal":1,"description":"Court of justice."},"speaker_label":null,"page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":67,"printed_page":66}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Court of Justice.","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u002","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d001","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"அனி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":67,"printed_page":66}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Socrates is a soul who should not be allowed to walk this country! That body which criticizes democratic government is filled with poison. With words that bind those who hear and language that even unwilling listeners desire, this base old man pours out harmful ideas and incites the young against the government, against God and against the law!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u003","kind":"stage-direction","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":null,"source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":{"kind":"stage-action","ordinal":2,"description":"Socrates laughs."},"speaker_label":null,"page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":67,"printed_page":66}]},"translation":{"english_text":"(Socrates laughs.)","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u004","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d002","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"நீதி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":67,"printed_page":66}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Why are you laughing? What is the reason?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u005","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d003","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":67,"printed_page":66}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Nothing, leader! In his anger Anitus forgets himself and mocks me as an old man. I remembered that and laughed. Both my head and Anitus's are grey as sea-foam! Isn't that so, members of the court? Anitus, isn't it true?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u006","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d004","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Socrates! I wish to remind you that this case and inquiry are not about the heads of you two!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u007","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d005","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Many thanks, Melitus! But just as the head is foremost in an eight-span body, the head is foremost in this case too. Knowledge flashes from my head; meaningless imaginings spring from your poet's head to destroy it; the arrogance of authority bursts from politician Anitus's head. This case is the result of the three-cornered battle among those three heads!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u008","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d006","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"See? He insults the law and the court! This is how he corrupted all the young people!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u009","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d007","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"How can an old man corrupt the young? Am I a courtesan casting a net for young men, a maiden offering the feast of youth?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u010","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d008","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"You possess enchanting speech women do not have, seductive ornamentation of words not even women who cast nets for young men possess—verbal magic, cascading phrases and many such arts.","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u011","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d009","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"You might as well call me a magician. Dear young man, in Athens I alone corrupt the young—is that it?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u012","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d010","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Yes!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u013","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d011","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"You don't corrupt them?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u014","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d012","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"No!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u015","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d013","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Anitus?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u016","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d014","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"No?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u017","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d015","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"The sculptor Lycon?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u018","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d016","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"No!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u019","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d017","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"This judge?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u020","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d018","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"No!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u021","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d019","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"So nobody corrupts the young; everyone does them good except me—is that right?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u022","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d020","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"மெலி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Yes... yes...","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u023","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d021","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":68,"printed_page":67}]},"translation":{"english_text":"When so many people—indeed the whole city of Athens—are setting the young right, how can I alone turn their path aside?","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u024","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d022","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"அனி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":69,"printed_page":68}]},"translation":{"english_text":"One drop of poison in a pot of milk!","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u025","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d023","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":69,"printed_page":68}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Or one lamp in a dark house! Why are you stunned, Melitus? Members of the court, the young circle me like skylarks not because of verbal ornament, but because of inexhaustible ideas, principles that do not lose their quality—thoughts precious as gold that this earth needs. They say I bewitch young hearts with beautiful language and layered phrases. But that language is not mine alone. I never forbade them from speaking it. Let them try too. Poor fellows—they spoke and lost, spoke and lost...","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u026","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d024","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"நீதி","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":69,"printed_page":68}]},"translation":{"english_text":"Socrates! Stop speaking; no explanation is needed. By the vote of the majority of this court, judgment is given that you must receive the sentence of death by drinking poison.","mode":"prose-faithful","notes":[]}},{"id":"raja-rani-en-s051-u027","kind":"dialogue","status":"verified","target_language":"en","scene_id":"raja-rani-s051","archival_scene_ordinal":51,"source":{"source_path":"works/raja-rani/dialogues/records/scene-051.json","canonical_scene_path":"works/raja-rani/scenes/scene-051.md","source_record_id":"raja-rani-s051-d025","source_occurrence_id":null,"source_locator":null,"speaker_label":"சாக்","page_provenance":[{"pdf_page":69,"printed_page":68},{"pdf_page":70,"printed_page":69}]},"translation":{"english_text":"The court of Greece gives its glorious judgment and smiles silently. In my short time I only tried to drive away the darkness that has long surrounded this country. The gift given to this old man, standing in the prisoner's dock for the first time after seventy years, is a heap of charges: Socrates does evil; he is an atheist who searches what is in heaven and earth; he twists falsehood into truth—and the sentence is death. I do not grieve. If death is like dreamless sleep, why should I fear it? If another world awaits after death, I may have the fortune to meet poets such as Homer and Pindar who went there before me. Friends who sentenced me, you will have to answer to the future. The youth of the future will not question as calmly as I do; sparks of fire will fly from their questions—heat, flood and storm will blow. After I, who held them to a peaceful path, am gone, selfish tigers, you will be unable to stand before the revolutionary volcano that will erupt; you will be shattered to pieces. History will turn. And if human beings are born again, in that birth too I will commit this same crime. I will do it again. This crime that pours honey through every atom of my thought—I will keep committing it everywhere, always, in every place!","english_page_segments":[{"pdf_page":69,"printed_page":68,"english_text":"The court of Greece gives its glorious judgment and smiles silently. In my short time I only tried to drive away the darkness that has long surrounded this country. The gift given to this old man, standing in the prisoner's dock for the first time after seventy years, is a heap of charges: Socrates does evil; he is an atheist who searches what is in heaven and earth; he twists falsehood into truth—and the sentence is death. I do not grieve. If death is like dreamless sleep, why should I fear it? If another world awaits after death, I may have the fortune to meet poets such as Homer and Pindar who went there before me. Friends who sentenced me, you will have to answer to the future. The youth of the future will not question as calmly as I do; sparks of fire will fly from their questions—heat, flood and storm will blow. After I, who held them to a peaceful path, am gone, selfish tigers, you will be unable to stand before the revolutionary volcano that will erupt; you will be shattered to pieces—"},{"pdf_page":70,"printed_page":69,"english_text":"you will be shattered to pieces. History will turn. And if human beings are born again, in that birth too I will commit this same crime. I will do it again. This crime that pours honey through every atom of my thought—I will keep committing it everywhere, always, in every place!"}],"mode":"prose-faithful","notes":["The final address crosses PDF 69→70 and remains one source-linked unit."]}}]}